 ],
 "literal": "Other than my dwelling town, the small hamlet having a shore / of the lord of the beautiful seashore grove, attributing cruelty, / suffering with unceasing sorrow, at midnight, / without sleeping, without conversing with those who dwell awake, / has a long night with only the people in the state of sleep.",
 "translation": "Other than my own native town, this small hamlet by the shore, belonging to the lord of the beautiful coastal groves, has an endlessly long night. Here, brooding over his cruelty, I suffer with unceasing sorrow, unable to converse with anyone who stays awake, surrounded only by people fast asleep.",
 "modern": "என் சொந்த ஊர் அல்லாத, கடற்கரைச் சோலைகளின் தலைவனுடைய கொடுமையை நினைத்து, நீங்காத துன்பத்துடன் வருந்தி, நள்ளிரவில் தூங்காமல் விழித்திருப்பவர்களுடன் பேசவும் முடியாமல், தூங்குகின்ற மக்களோடு மட்டும் கழிக்கின்ற நீண்ட இரவைக் கொண்டது இந்தச் சிறிய கடற்கரைக்குடி.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to herself or her confidante during a sleepless night of separation. She laments that the small coastal hamlet where she is staying, which is not her native town, feels incredibly lonely. She broods over the indifference of her lover, the lord of the maritime tract, which causes her unceasing sorrow. In the deep silence of midnight, she cannot find anyone awake to talk to and share her grief. Surrounded only by sleeping villagers, she is left entirely alone with her painful thoughts, making the night feel endlessly long. This setting of a quiet, isolated seashore hamlet at night perfectly captures the theme of anxious waiting and despair characteristic of the Neytal landscape.",
